Project Summary
Project Title: Move & Learn: Motor Skills Development App
Course: Human-Computer Interaction (HCI)
Team Size: 4 students
Project Type: Group Assignment / Final Project
The Move & Learn app is a motion-controlled game designed to improve motor skills for children with physical disabilities. The project was developed using a deeply human-centered design approach, grounded in the real experiences and needs of children and teens facing challenges with eye-hand coordination. The project included needfinding through in-person observations, interviews with caregivers and therapists, and direct user testing with children. Multiple design iterations were carried out, including storyboards, personas, prototypes, heuristic evaluations, and usability testing. The final interactive prototype encourages repeated physical movement, offers audio-visual rewards, and adapts to individual user progress, creating an engaging and accessible experience for its users.
My contributions to the project included note-taking and project organization, where I documented weekly meeting progress and ensured alignment with deadlines. I also took part in final editing by reviewing and refining deliverables for clarity and completeness. In addition, I supported the user-centered design process by assisting in the development of personas, storyboards, and prototype testing. Throughout the project, I collaborated closely with team members, coordinating tasks and ensuring that all assignments were completed efficiently.
The project utilized Adobe XD and Figma for prototype design, motion tracking and gesture-based controls for interactive functionality, and video demonstrations such as Balloon Game.mov. Collaborative tools such as Google Docs and Google Classroom were used for planning and communication.
The outcome of the project was a fully functional prototype that was tested with real users. User feedback was incorporated to improve accessibility, motivation, and engagement. The final design demonstrates a balance between technical functionality and empathy-driven, inclusive design principles, reflecting both the practical and human-centered aspects of Human-Computer Interaction.


